\begin{abstract}
Bengali-speaking users face two unresolved problems with existing screen readers. No major tool handles Bengali-English code-switching natively which forces users to switch languages manually or accept unintelligible output. Separately, dynamic interfaces such as Zoom, WhatsApp, Browser applications generate high-frequency UI Automation event bursts that fragment speech and spike CPU usage under existing architectures. We present ALO, a plugin-based intelligent screen reader for Windows that addresses both problems. A three-stage bilingual pipeline handles language routing: Unicode NFKC normalization, sliding-window script tokenization and a 5,000-entry Romanized Bengali lexicon together route each text segment to the correct synthesis engine automatically. A priority-stratified temporal event coalescer suppresses redundant UI Automation events within 50\,ms windows before they reach the speech pipeline, while a pre-indexed virtual buffer enables $O(\log n)$ navigation without live UIA queries. A deterministic two-tier COM lifecycle tracker eliminates the memory leaks common in long-running Windows accessibility clients. Benchmarked against NVDA and JAWS across three hardware configurations, ALO achieved 8--11\% lower event-to-speech latency against NVDA, a 3.7:1 event reduction ratio and 14.5\% lower average CPU load, at the cost of a higher memory footprint of 300-400\,MB from the pre-loaded UIA cache. In a task-based study with 15 visually impaired users in Bangladesh, ALO demonstrated consistent advantages over NVDA and JAWS across all task categories, with the most pronounced differences on Bengali and mixed-language tasks where NVDA's 134.6\,ms per-boundary engine-switching overhead compounded into perceptible lag that ALO's 105.0\,ms end-to-end switching cost reduced meaningfully.
\end{abstract}

\begin{keywords}
Accessibility, assistive technology, bilingual text-to-speech, Bengali language processing, event processing, human-computer interaction, Romanized Bengali, screen readers, software architecture, visual impairment, Windows UI Automation.
\end{keywords}

