\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Visually impaired people rely on screen readers for almost everything they do on a computer, from reading documents to filling in forms to following a conversation in a group chat \cite{manirajee2024assistive}. Tools like JAWS \cite{jaws_screen_reader} and NVDA \cite{nvda_screen_reader} have gotten very good at this under straightforward conditions. The trouble is that conditions are rarely straightforward anymore. Modern applications update their interfaces constantly, push notifications in rapid bursts and carry content in multiple languages, particularly languages outside the English-dominant mainstream that these tools were built around \cite{hoffman2005emerging}.

Bengali users sit at an uncomfortable intersection of these problems. Everyday communication in Bangladesh routinely mixes Bengali and English within the same sentence, whether in a workplace message, a university assignment or a social media post and no major screen reader handles that natively \cite{ahmed2017ifreephony, era2026evaluating, motahar2025accessibility}. Routing mixed content through English-only TTS engines produces speech that is often completely unintelligible and requiring users to manually switch languages mid-task treats the symptom rather than the underlying problem \cite{sitaram2019survey}.

A separate technical problem compounds this. Active applications like Zoom can trigger hundreds of UI Automation events per second from chat updates and roster changes alone \cite{hinze2009event}. When these pile up, speech fragments mid-sentence, the same text repeats and the CPU spikes. Beyond event volume, monolithic screen reader cores force a meeting platform and a spreadsheet through the same processing pipeline, making it genuinely hard to improve behaviour for one application without breaking something in another \cite{jain2025automated}.

Three research questions follow directly from these gaps: (1) How can a screen reader support Bengali-English code-switching, including Romanized Bengali, without requiring manual language management? (2) How can event processing reduce speech interruptions while retaining important information? (3) How can a flexible system support app-specific accessibility without changing the main system?

To answer these questions, we introduce ALO, a plugin-based screen reader built around Bengali-English bilingual interaction. The system handles Bengali, English and Banglish, the informal mix of Bengali and English scripts common in digital communication in Bangladesh, through a three-stage pipeline: Unicode NFKC normalization and script-based tokenization to segment mixed-language strings, a 5,000-entry Romanized Bengali lexicon to convert phonetically written Bengali into its canonical orthographic form and dual-engine routing to dispatch each text segment to the correct synthesis backend without user intervention. A priority-aware coalescing mechanism reduces speech fragmentation from high-frequency UI Automation streams at the source, rather than relying solely on queuing or interruption. The plugin architecture allows application-specific modules to be developed and deployed independently of the core system. ALO also incorporates a guided onboarding mechanism, motivated directly by what we observed during early deployments: new users were not overwhelmed by the feature depth, but by the sheer volume of commands arriving all at once. Rather than presenting users with a comprehensive command list upfront, the system surfaces hotkeys and navigation patterns incrementally through context-sensitive audio cues as they explore the interface.

Throughout development, blind and low-vision users in Bangladesh participated in extended testing across the core plugins. Recurring feedback on speech clarity, language switching reliability and disorienting interaction patterns was treated as genuine design signal; the guided onboarding mechanism and the prioritization of WhatsApp and Zoom plugins both emerged directly from this process.

The main contributions of this paper are: (i) a three-stage bilingual text-processing pipeline with Unicode normalization, sliding-window tokenization and Romanized Bengali lexicon matching; (ii) a priority-stratified temporal event coalescing mechanism that reduces speech interruptions by a factor of 3.7:1 under typical workloads; (iii) a sandboxed plugin architecture enabling independent development of application-specific readers; (iv) a deterministic COM lifecycle tracker eliminating long-running memory leaks; and (v) comprehensive empirical benchmarking against NVDA and JAWS confirming 8--25\% performance improvements across latency, resource and bilingual processing metrics. The application installer, source code and user manual are provided as supplementary materials (Section~2)\footref{fn:supplementary}.

