\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

For over two decades the dominant screen reader architecture has followed a shared model: intercept operating system events, translate interface elements into text and route the result through speech synthesis \cite{raman2012auditory}. JAWS \cite{jaws_screen_reader} and NVDA \cite{nvda_screen_reader} each refined this pipeline and perform reliably in stable, well-structured environments. Successive generations of dynamic applications have steadily exposed the limits of that model and research has tried to address them. Early systems read from character buffers directly \cite{thatcher1994screen}, an approach that held up until graphical interfaces arrived and broke the assumption that in-memory text was a reliable map of the visual display. Off-Screen Models were the next attempt to reconstruct that map from rendered output, though they proved noisy and fragile in non-trivial interfaces \cite{mynatt1994nonvisual}. The introduction of MSAA and, later, UI Automation gave screen readers a structured, programmatic window into interface state, which was a meaningful step forward \cite{asakawa1998user}, but the cross-process overhead of querying these APIs introduced latency that users could actually perceive \cite{nino2024assistive}. Virtual buffers addressed this by caching the interface tree locally \cite{bigham2008webanywhere}, yet they depend on well-structured UI trees that many locally developed applications in Bangladesh simply never produce. Work studying how blind users actually move through auditory interfaces \cite{edwards1989modelling, zhao2007earpod} has shaped how feedback is designed and more recent efforts around context-sensitive guidance \cite{gonzalez2025tapnav} and LLM-driven interaction \cite{kodandaram2024enabling} suggest the field is still looking for better answers. What the research keeps returning to is that finding your way through an interface without sight is genuinely hard and the friction has not gone away \cite{jordan2024information}.

The Bengali side of this history tells a different story. Early tools like the Festival-based Bengali screen reader and Mongol Dip showed that bilingual screen reading was possible in principle \cite{amin2012bilingual, khan2014mongoldip, nahar2015design}, but neither could hold up under the demands of real daily use the performance was too slow and the code-switching too unreliable. What is telling is how users responded: studies of South Asian screen reader adoption show that people switch tools based on which one handles their language, not out of any preference for the tool itself \cite{mccarthy2013voice}. That pattern makes the gap feel less like a technical footnote and more like an unmet need that has been quietly shaping behaviour for years. Research in the Bengali assistive technology space has moved steadily forward from OCR and Braille systems \cite{rajbongshi2020bangla, sojib2018braille} to neural TTS and image captioning \cite{ghosh2020assistive, bijoy2025bangla, khan2022bancap}, with NLP work opening further directions \cite{roy2024question} but none of it has brought event load management, bilingual synthesis and a plugin architecture together in one system. Getting Bengali and English to coexist reliably within a single synthesized sentence, in a real-time screen reader, remains an open problem \cite{sitaram2019survey, islam2025review}.

On the event processing side, prior work has addressed high-frequency UI event streams through queuing or interruption \cite{eugster2003many, hinze2009event}, but both approaches manage the backlog after it forms rather than preventing redundant events from entering the pipeline. The structural limitation of monolithic screen reader cores where a meeting platform and a spreadsheet share the same processing path has been identified as a persistent barrier to application-specific accessibility improvements \cite{jain2025automated}, yet scripting-based extensions remain the prevailing workaround. Systems that have addressed performance tend to do so for monolingual, English-dominant contexts; those targeting Bengali accessibility have not attempted event management or extensible plugin architectures. ALO is designed to close that gap.

