\section{System Architecture}
\label{sec:architecture}

\subsection{Architectural Overview}

ALO was developed across four structured milestones covering architecture design, bilingual processing, plugin integration and system evaluation; the full milestone breakdown is provided in supplementary Section~3\footref{fn:supplementary}. The system is structured as a layered pipeline in which each stage has a single, well-defined responsibility. Raw UI Automation events enter at the bottom; redundant and duplicate signals are discarded before they propagate further. The remaining events are grouped and time-filtered through the coalescing stage to suppress high-frequency noise that would otherwise fragment speech. Filtered events are then translated into navigable units by the virtual buffer, which presents the navigation layer with a clean, indexed representation of the active interface rather than the raw UIA tree. The navigation layer exposes only the elements relevant to the current user action, keeping cognitive load low. Figure~\ref{fig:sys_arch_layers} shows this layered organisation.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../diagrams/system_architecture.pdf}
\caption{System Architecture detailing the layered interaction from multimodal I/O to cognitive navigation heuristics.}
\label{fig:sys_arch_layers}
\end{figure}

The design prioritizes cognitive load reduction \cite{kalyuga2011cognitive, shneiderman2003eyes}, perceptual latency, extensibility and language parity. Every cross-process path is optimized to keep audio response within the threshold of comfortable human perception; application-specific behavior is isolated in self-contained plugins; and Bengali is handled natively throughout the pipeline rather than bolted on after the fact.

\subsection{Core Framework and Plugin-Based Design}

Application-specific behavior is encapsulated in dynamically loaded plugins. Fig.~\ref{fig:arch} shows how initialization builds a dependency injection container that wires the core framework, shared services and plugin modules into a single runtime. This keeps the core stable while new plugins extend it independently.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../diagrams/high_level_architecture.png}
\caption{High-Level Plugin Architecture. The system bootstrap initializes the dependency injection container, which wires the core framework, reader plugins and service layer into a unified, extensible structure.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

At runtime, \texttt{Desktop\-Event\-Manager} monitors foreground application changes and triggers \texttt{Program\-Reader\-Factory} to load the appropriate plugin. The component relationships are illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:plugin_layer}; each plugin is self-contained and communicates with shared services such as UI Automation and text-to-speech through a common interface. Application-specific logic remains isolated within each plugin but the core system acts consistently regardless of which application is active.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../diagrams/plugin_architecture.png}
\caption{Plugin Architecture where the DesktopEventManager activates the ProgramReaderFactory to generate specific readings of the plugin.}
\label{fig:plugin_layer}
\end{figure}

Each plugin follows a strict lifecycle, illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:plugin_discovery}: assembly load, factory registration, instantiation, initialization, active event processing and finally disposal. Plugins subscribe to events only within their own application context and are torn down cleanly when the user switches away or the plugin is no longer needed. Keeping each module responsible for its own cleanup means resources are released predictably, application contexts stay isolated from one another and the system remains stable even under rapid application switching.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../diagrams/plugin_architecture_flow.png}
\caption{Plugin Lifecycle States. This state diagram shows the robust initialization, active event processing and deterministic disposal phases of application-specific readers.}
\label{fig:plugin_discovery}
\end{figure}

