Keyboard events go through the same pipeline as everything else. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:event_priority}, user actions are not separate; they directly affect what is already in progress. They update the navigation state and also change what gets spoken next. When working with Windows applications, we also have to deal with COM objects and if they are not managed carefully, they can quietly cause memory leaks over time \cite{box1998essential}. In Figure~\ref{fig:com_tracker}, we document the process of tracking and releasing the COM object within ALO. We have classified objects into 2 types: long-lived (primary) and short-lived (secondary). Primary objects are released as soon as their associated plugin unloads or the application context shifts. Secondary objects are periodically swept during low-activity windows rather than held until shutdown. This two-tier release strategy eliminates the memory leaks and dangling object references that routinely appear in COM-based integrations left to framework defaults \cite{hertz2005quantifying}.

