\end{figure}

\subsection{Event Processing and Temporal Coalescing}

Processing every UI Automation event as it arrives without any filtering produces a stream of speech interruptions that is practically unusable under real workloads \cite{welsh2001seda}. ALO addresses this through a temporal coalescing mechanism (Algorithm~\ref{alg:coalesce}) that groups events arriving within the same 50~ms window before deciding what to emit. Events are basically sorted into three priority queues: focus, structure and property. Common events in the window are merged and only the relevant update is emitted. Focus events will always bubble up. Critical events bypass coalescing altogether and are delivered immediately.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Filtering Events}
\label{alg:coalesce}
\begin{algorithmic}
\STATE \textbf{Input:} Stream $S$ of raw events
\STATE \textbf{Output:} Stream $E$ of coalesced events for speech
\STATE \textbf{Definition:} $\delta_{window} = 50\text{ms}$ (coalescing window)
\STATE $Q_{focus} \gets PriorityQueue(High)$
\STATE $Q_{structure} \gets PriorityQueue(Medium)$
\STATE $Q_{property} \gets PriorityQueue(Low)$
\WHILE{$SystemRunning$}
 \IF{Event $e$ arrives}
 \IF{$e.Type == Focus$}
 \STATE $Q_{focus}.Enqueue(e)$
 \ELSIF{$e.Type == Structure$}
 \STATE $Q_{structure}.Enqueue(e)$
 \ELSE
 \STATE $Q_{property}.Enqueue(e)$
 \ENDIF
 \ENDIF
 \IF{$Timer > \delta_{window}$}
 \STATE \COMMENT{Flush Buffers in priority order}
 \IF{!$Q_{focus}.IsEmpty()$}
 \STATE $e_{latest} \gets Q_{focus}.Last()$
 \STATE $E \gets E \cup \{e_{latest}\}$
 \STATE $Q_{focus}.Clear()$ \COMMENT{Drop redundant focus events}
 \ELSIF{!$Q_{structure}.IsEmpty()$}
 \STATE $E \gets E \cup ProcessBatch(Q_{structure})$
 \ELSIF{!$Q_{property}.IsEmpty()$}
 \STATE $E \gets E \cup ProcessBatch(Q_{property})$
 \COMMENT{Emit latest property update only}
 \ENDIF
 \STATE $ResetTimer()$
 \ENDIF
\ENDWHILE
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Virtual Buffer and Navigation Pipeline}

To reduce the latency associated with repeated UI tree traversal, the system employs a virtual buffer that converts hierarchical UI structures into a linear representation \cite{bigham2008webanywhere}. We show in Fig.~\ref{fig:uia_architecture_flow} how raw UI events are turned into navigable elements through filtering, caching and indexing in a single pipeline.

\begin{figure*}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../diagrams/uia_architecture_navigation_flow.png}
\caption{End-to-End UI Automation Navigation Flow}
\label{fig:uia_architecture_flow}
\end{figure*}

The virtual buffer flattens the UI element hierarchy and maintains a sorted index over common structural types headings, links and table cells enabling navigation to any of these at $O(\log n)$ time without re-querying the UI Automation tree. When the buffer provides sufficient structural clarity, the navigation layer reads directly from it. When the interface is irregular or sparsely annotated, the system falls back to lightweight heuristic inference rather than failing silently.

\subsection{Event Capture, State Management and Concurrency}

Keyboard input is processed by an input manager that translates raw key events into semantic navigation commands, which are tightly coupled with the event pipeline, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:event_priority}. ALO tracks a centralized application state-focused element, hierarchical context and navigation position and updates them in response to both UI events and user actions (Figs.~\ref{fig:uia_focus} and~\ref{fig:focus_change_flow}). Navigation remains consistent even through rapid context changes. User preferences persist across sessions. Event processing, navigation and speech synthesis run on separate threads. Hazard pointers and cancellation tokens manage shared resource access \cite{michael2004hazard, watson2018writing}. When a higher-priority event arrives, it cancels ongoing lower-priority speech to keep the output current. We run speech synthesis and language identification on background threads so they do not interfere with each other.

\subsection{Bilingual Text-to-Speech Processing}

When text arrives at the synthesis stage, it may be Bengali, English or a mix of both. Fig.~\ref{fig:tts_flow} shows how the pipeline routes each segment to the correct engine.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../diagrams/tts_flow.png}
\caption{Text-To-Speech (TTS) routing flow.}
\label{fig:tts_flow}
\end{figure}

We begin by verifying each text segment for the presence of Bengali characters within the Unicode range U+0980-U+09FF \cite{islam2025review}. That works for clear cases. Not always, though. Mixed or transliterated text needs something else, so we rely on a lightweight identifier that looks at context and picks the right synthesis path using regional data \cite{ahmad2021sust, sultana2021bangla, samin2024bangladialecto}. Once that decision is made, we use neural synthesis for both Bengali and English, which improves pronunciation and also reduces switching delay \cite{bhattacharjee2021end, liu2025lao, ghosh2025medibeng}. Bengali synthesis is heavier, so we do not keep it on the main thread. We offload it through a WebSocket bridge. The C\# side sends text out and continues; the audio comes back later.

Speech prosody is adjusted using SSML markup: headings receive elevated pitch and rate to signal structural prominence, while metadata is rendered at a softer pitch. ALO's priority-aware queue handles preemption by pausing the current utterance, storing its position and resuming after the higher-priority output completes. Time-critical control states such as microphone or camera changes in Zoom are announced immediately, with a deferred background verification to confirm state accuracy. Application identity cannot always be inferred from window class alone; many Electron-based applications share identical class names, so process-level differentiation is applied before any shortcut bindings are activated to prevent cross-application interference.

