\section{Bilingual Text Processing and Language Routing}
\label{sec:bilingual}

Mixed Bengali-English content appears throughout the digital environments used in Bangladesh. Bengali and English text regularly coexist within the same interface element, notification, or message thread. Beyond script-mixed content, Romanized Bengali text written in Latin script words such as ``ami,'' ``tumi,'' ``valo,'' or ``kemon acho'' is widespread in casual messaging and social media. Standard screen readers treat all such content as monolingual English, routing it through English synthesis engines and producing output that is either severely mispronounced or entirely unintelligible.

\subsection{Unicode-Based Language Detection}

The first stage of the language routing pipeline uses Unicode codepoint analysis to carry out script-based language detection. Every text segment extracted from a UI Automation element is first normalized using Unicode NFKC normalization, which standardizes character representations, eliminates superfluous whitespace and control characters and resolves compatibility equivalences that arise from differences in application display and input handling. Emojis and symbolic characters are preserved during normalization but excluded from language classification; they are passed directly to the downstream synthesis engine.

Following normalization, the detection system divides characters into three main categories: Bengali script characters in the Unicode range U+0980-U+09FF; Latin script characters in the ranges U+0041-U+007A and extended Latin blocks; and numeric characters together with punctuation symbols. A sliding-window tokenization technique is then applied to segment the text by grouping consecutive characters that belong to the same script category into contiguous tokens. This segmentation allows mixed-language content appearing inside a single interface element to be separated into smaller, language-consistent pieces before routing.

For instance, the interface string ``[Bengali-script token] payment successful'' produces two voice units. The Bengali-script segment is recognized as Bengali text and sent to the Bengali speech synthesis pipeline, whereas the English part ``payment successful'' is routed to the English voice synthesis pipeline. A brief pause is inserted between segments to reduce the perceptual gap between voices.

\subsection{Romanized Bengali Detection and Lexicon Matching}

Romanized Bengali writing, which uses Latin script to write Bengali words such as ``ami,'' ``tumi,'' ``valo,'' or ``kemon acho,'' is a frequent challenge in digital communication in Bangladesh. Unicode script recognition alone is not a reliable method for interpreting such material, since these words appear as ordinary Latin characters. ALO incorporates a lexicon-based Romanized Bengali recognition module to solve this issue. The system's curated dictionary contains approximately 5,000 commonly used Bengali terms and their frequently seen Romanized equivalents.

The lexicon was constructed by combining publicly accessible Bengali dictionaries, frequently used transliteration patterns and manually selected items from informal communication corpora including messaging and social media content. Each lexicon entry maps one or more Romanized surface forms to a canonical Bengali word representation. For instance, the Romanized form ``ami'' is mapped to its Bengali-script equivalent; variants ``valo'' and ``bhalo'' are both mapped to the same Bengali lexical form; and the token ``tumi'' corresponds to its Bengali-script form.

During processing, Latin-script tokens produced by the segmentation stage are compared against the Romanized lexicon. If a match is found, the token value is replaced with its canonical Bengali orthographic representation and forwarded to the Bengali text-to-speech pipeline. Tokens for which no lexicon match exists are routed to the English synthesis engine as English text. This lexicon-based approach effectively recognizes commonly recurring Romanized Bengali patterns. It eliminates the computational expense of phonetic modeling or machine-learning-based transliteration systems, hence it is suitable for real-time screen reader operation.

\subsection{Mixed-Language Speech Synthesis}

Following language detection and Romanized Bengali normalization, each segment is dispatched to the appropriate synthesis backend. Bengali segments are synthesized by the Bengali text-to-speech engine; English segments are processed by the English text-to-speech engine. ALO switches between synthesis engines at segment boundaries established by punctuation and script transitions. Brief pauses are inserted between segments to reduce the perceptual discontinuity between voices.

This segmentation approach generates understandable voice output for mixed Bengali-English content in the majority of interface circumstances, including notifications, form labels and menu items. Sophisticated intra-sentence code-switching remains a known limitation of the current system, especially when Romanized Bengali, acronyms and English proper names appear in the same phrase. Numerical content is routed based on the dominant linguistic context of its surrounding segment: when numbers appear in Bengali-dominant segments, Bengali speech conventions are used; otherwise, English pronunciation is applied.

