\section{Windows Accessibility Support}
\label{sec:windows_accessibility}

Personal computers remain the primary platform for productivity and digital participation and screen readers are the main interface through which visually impaired users access them \cite{manirajee2024assistive}. ALO is built directly on top of the Windows UI Automation framework, running as a background accessibility client that watches for interface changes, responds to keyboard input and produces audio feedback in real time \cite{asakawa1998user}. Rather than treating Bengali speech support as an add-on, we designed dedicated components for language-aware text processing and interaction patterns that reflect how Bangladeshi users actually communicate and navigate \cite{jain2025automated}.

\subsection{Windows UI Automation Service Integration}

Windows UI Automation gives ALO its structured view into running applications. On startup, ALO registers three event handlers with the UIA subsystem: \texttt{Automation\-Focus\-Changed\-Event\-Handler} for focus movement, \texttt{Automation\-Property\-Changed\-Event\-Handler} for value updates such as a button label or checkbox state and \texttt{Automation\-Structure\-Changed\-Event\-Handler} for elements being added or removed. Between them these three handlers cover essentially everything that changes in a live Windows interface.

Navigation is built around \texttt{Automation\-Element}. ALO calls \texttt{Get\-Focused\-Element()} to track current focus and \texttt{Find\-All()} with a \texttt{Tree\-Scope} and a condition to walk subtrees. Control interaction uses the standard UIA pattern interfaces \texttt{Invoke\-Pattern} for buttons, \texttt{Selection\-Pattern} for lists and \texttt{Value\-Pattern} for editable fields with callbacks delivered through \texttt{IUI\-Automation\-Event\-Handler} on a background thread. During startup, ALO configures event scope, builds the initial navigation index and wires audio feedback before any event is processed. Fig.~\ref{fig:startup_flow} shows the startup sequence and Fig.~\ref{fig:uia_focus} illustrates how a focus change flows through the system.

\subsection{Interaction with the Accessibility Tree}

The accessibility tree provides a structured map of every UI element currently on screen. ALO traverses this tree using the \texttt{AutomationElement} model to collect element names, roles, states and actionable properties. Research on UI automation testing \cite{feng2024enabling} and vision-language UI understanding \cite{li2022spotlight} has demonstrated how rich the UIA tree can be as a source of structured interface information; ALO exploits the same structure but for real-time screen reader navigation rather than test generation. Since many accessibility failures in Windows applications come from missing or poor-quality metadata unlabeled controls, images without names, or dynamically generated content from frameworks such as Flutter, React Native, or Electron the traversal needs to be both thorough and fast \cite{jain2025automated}.

\subsubsection{Efficient Tree Crawl Using CacheRequest}

Reading a property from a UI Automation element is not free each read crosses a process boundary as a COM call. If you do that naively, a screen with 100 elements and seven properties each means 700 separate roundtrips before you can even build the navigation index. ALO avoids this by setting up a \texttt{CacheRequest} before every crawl. The request declares exactly which properties are needed \texttt{Name}, \texttt{Control\-Type}, \texttt{Automation\-Id}, \texttt{Is\-Enabled}, \texttt{Is\-Offscreen}, \texttt{Localized\-Control\-Type} and \texttt{Bounding\-Rectangle} so when \texttt{Automation\-Element.\-Find\-All()} runs with that request attached, the UIA provider fetches everything in one roundtrip and hands back fully populated objects. A typical Windows application with 80-120 visible elements goes from several hundred cross-process calls down to one. This is where most of ALO's traversal speed advantage over NVDA comes from, since NVDA's Python-based COM interop reads each property individually through \texttt{pywin32}.

\subsubsection{Selective Retrieval with Condition Filtering}

Pulling every element out of the subtree and then throwing most of them away in C\# code wastes both the transfer cost and the allocation. Instead, ALO builds an \texttt{OrCondition} from pre-allocated \texttt{PropertyCondition} objects covering only the control types that matter for navigation buttons, edits, text blocks, hyperlinks, list items, menu items, checkboxes and headings and passes it directly to \texttt{Find\-All(\-Tree\-Scope.\-Subtree,\allowbreak{} condition,\allowbreak{} cache\-Request)}. UIA evaluates this filter on the provider side, before any data moves across the process boundary, so irrelevant elements never reach the screen reader process at all. The \texttt{PropertyCondition} objects themselves are created once when the service starts and reused on every subsequent crawl.

\subsubsection{Background Thread Build and Visual-Order Indexing}

When the foreground application changes, the crawl goes onto a background thread immediately, so the main event thread stays free to announce the focus change and handle any keystrokes that come in while the tree is being walked. Once \texttt{FindAll} comes back, the collected elements get sorted by the top-left corner of their \texttt{BoundingRectangle} this puts them in the same order a sighted person would read the screen top-to-bottom, left-to-right, rather than whatever order the application happens to insert them into the tree. That sorted list is then split by \texttt{ControlType} into indexed buckets, so a query like ``jump to next heading'' is a binary search on a small list rather than a scan of everything. Traversal stops at $m = 250$ elements to keep things predictable on interfaces that have unusually deep or wide trees.

\subsubsection{Partial Cache Invalidation on StructureChanged Events}

In something like Zoom or WhatsApp the tree changes constantly new messages arrive, participant lists update, status indicators flip. Rebuilding the whole buffer every time would make the system thrash. Instead, ALO hooks \texttt{Automation\-Structure\-Changed\-Event\-Handler}, which hands back the exact subtree root that changed and a \texttt{Structure\-Change\-Type} telling you whether children were added, removed, or rearranged. ALO re-crawls just that subtree, splices the new entries into the existing index and removes anything that is no longer there. Everything else in the buffer stays as it was. When only a property value changes say a button label updates \texttt{Automation\-Property\-Changed\-Event\-Handler} patches that single cached entry directly, with no traversal needed at all.

Once the buffer is live, every navigation query next heading, next link, next form field is answered from the in-memory index without touching UIA. When metadata is missing or the tree is too sparse to be useful, as happens regularly with Electron or applications that draw their own controls, the plugin layer reads what it can from raw text content and reconstructs enough structure to keep navigation working.

