\section{Interaction, Navigation and Application Support}
\label{sec:interaction}

ALO surfaces hotkeys and navigation patterns incrementally through context-sensitive audio cues rather than presenting users with a comprehensive command list upfront, a design that emerged directly from early user feedback.

Table~\ref{tab:hotkeys} summarizes the hotkey sets provided by each plugin. The Zoom plugin illustrates the depth that this model enables: 21 purpose-built shortcuts cover core meeting functionality including audio, video, chat, participant management and reactions. Rather than tabbing through interface elements to reach a specific chat message, users can navigate directly by sender, date or message content and compose replies without losing position in the conversation. Facebook presented a different problem: generic browser shortcuts kept colliding with what ALO needed to do, so the Facebook reader defines 9 commands tied specifically to social media tasks, giving each action its own binding rather than fighting over keys the browser already claims. Word and Excel were about a different kind of friction long documents and wide spreadsheets where arrowing through everything line by line is genuinely painful. Structured navigation lets users jump straight to a heading or a table column header, which in practice cuts down the time spent hunting for a specific section considerably \cite{zong2022rich}.

