\section{Evaluation Methodology}
\label{sec:eval_method}

We carried out empirical studies that measured system responsiveness, efficient use of resources, voice synthesis performance and user task performance in order to assess the efficacy of the ALO screen reader design. The assessment integrated human-centered usability tests with automated system benchmarks to evaluate both technical functionality and practical accessibility outcomes. Detailed user study data and benchmark logs are provided in supplementary Section~5.\footnote{\label{fn:supplementary}The supplementary document is available at: \url{https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/12lT9ZsCfsiYIaH9AekMP_I5HmBZ1IJtA?usp=sharing}}

\subsection{Study A: Participatory Design Evaluation}

Before running any controlled comparisons, we wanted to hear directly from visually impaired users about what was and was not working. This first stage brought participants into the design process rather than treating them as subjects at the end of it, with the goal of gathering honest, grounded feedback on everyday usability and on how well ALO handled Bengali-language content in practice.

\subsubsection{Participant Recruitment}

Participants were recruited through local accessibility advocacy networks and assistive technology communities in Dhaka, Bangladesh. Recruitment criteria included complete blindness or severe visual impairment, regular computer use, familiarity with at least one screen reader and fluency in Bengali. Participants represented varied professional and educational backgrounds to enable diverse usage perspectives. Eight participants took part in this phase. The cohort was intentionally small, consistent with the formative design-review purpose of this stage; results are interpreted qualitatively rather than statistically.

\subsubsection{Workshop Structure}

Each participant took part in two standard sessions, each lasting approximately two hours, held at the Bangladesh Computer Council accessibility laboratory. The first session focused on feature exploration and usability assessment. In the first session, participants used ALO to do things they would normally do on a computer reading notifications, moving through application menus, typing messages that mixed Bengali and English and browsing Bengali websites. As they worked, we asked them to speak their thoughts out loud so we could hear where things felt natural and where they got stuck. The second session was more of a conversation. Participants came back having already tried the system, so we could have a direct discussion about what bothered them things like which keyboard shortcuts felt unintuitive, whether the speech rate was right and how reliably the system switched between Bengali and English.

\subsubsection{Data Collection}

We conducted all study procedures under a protocol reviewed and approved by Apurba Technologies Ltd.\ before any participant was approached. We are grateful to EBLICT, Bangladesh for their logistical support in facilitating participant access and session coordination. Before data collection began, we obtained written informed consent from every participant and made clear that withdrawal at any point carried no consequence and that all responses would be anonymized prior to analysis. During sessions, we collected observational notes, conducted informal interviews and administered formal feature assessments. Participants rated system features on a 10-point satisfaction scale and shared open-ended qualitative comments. We audio-recorded all feedback sessions with participant consent and applied thematic coding to identify recurring patterns across responses.

\subsection{Study B: Controlled Task-Based Comparison}

The second stage was a controlled within-subject comparison of ALO against NVDA and JAWS, using standardized tasks designed to reflect real usage scenarios for visually impaired Bangladeshi users.

\subsubsection{Participants}

Through Dhaka's community networks and accessibility groups, fifteen visually impaired individuals were recruited. All participants reported using NVDA, JAWS, or comparable assistive technology as their primary screen reader and had an average of 4.2 years of screen reader experience. The age range was 21 to 47 years (mean = 32.6 years). All participants were fluent Bengali speakers who could independently carry out typical computer interaction tasks.

\subsubsection{Experimental Design}

All participants used three screen readers ALO, NVDA and JAWS to perform an identical set of tasks as part of a within-subject experimental design. The order was counterbalanced among participants to reduce order effects. Each participant received a short orientation session introducing ALO's navigation commands and shortcuts before task execution.

\subsubsection{Task Design}

Evaluation tasks were designed to represent common Windows computing scenarios that visually impaired Bangladeshi users encounter. Tasks covered three categories: (i) \textit{document navigation}, requiring participants to locate and read specific sections within a multi-heading Microsoft Word document; (ii) \textit{mixed-language text input}, requiring participants to compose a message with a Bengali subject line and mixed Bengali-English body text; and (iii) \textit{Bengali web browsing}, requiring participants to locate specific information within a Bengali government service portal. Each task had well-defined start and completion conditions.

\subsubsection{Evaluation Metrics}

Three metrics assessed system performance: task completion time in seconds, number of interaction errors and total interaction count. Interaction errors included incorrect navigational actions, inadvertent activation of interface components and failures to locate required interface elements. Interaction counts included focus changes, activation commands and keyboard navigation inputs recorded throughout task execution.

\subsection{System Benchmarking Methodology}

System benchmarking tests were carried out across the three hardware configurations shown in Table~\ref{tab:test_env}. Performance metrics included event-to-speech latency, navigation latency, event processing throughput, CPU utilization, memory consumption and battery impact. Event timestamps were recorded using \texttt{Stopwatch.\-Get\-Timestamp()} and \texttt{DateTime.\-Utc\-Now} via the high-precision .NET timing APIs. Speech output start timings were captured via \texttt{Speak\-Progress\-Changed} TTS callbacks. Resource consumption measurements were collected using Windows Performance Monitor counters (\texttt{Process\-Counter}, \texttt{Memory\-Counter}) and \texttt{Performance\-Counter} diagnostics during controlled navigation workloads.

