\section{Empirical Assessment}
\label{sec:empirical}

We evaluated three aspects of system behaviour that matter most in practice: how quickly the system responds to accessibility events, how it handles keyboard input and how it holds up under sustained use. Each reflects a real cost for blind users a slow response or a CPU spike during a long session is not a benchmark number, it is a broken interaction.

\subsection{Navigation and Keyboard Input Latency}

Navigation latency is the time interval between detection of a keyboard input event and execution of the corresponding accessibility action. For non-visual navigation to be responsive, low latency is necessary. The mean latency for single keystroke navigation is 71.4\,ms ($\sigma = 12.6$\,ms) on Configuration~C and 58.3\,ms ($\sigma = 9.4$\,ms) on Configuration~A. For arrow-key directional navigation, slightly higher latency is observed due to focus traversal processing, averaging 84.7\,ms ($\sigma = 14.3$\,ms) on Configuration~C and 69.2\,ms ($\sigma = 10.8$\,ms) on Configuration~A. These figures are comparable to NVDA on equivalent hardware; the advantage comes under sustained dynamic load where the coalescing mechanism and indexed virtual buffer reduce cumulative processing overhead. Across all three hardware configurations, the 95th percentile latency stayed under 140\,ms, within acceptable bounds for screen reader interaction (Table~\ref{tab:perfcomp}).

\subsection{Accessibility Event Processing Throughput}

The system's capacity to handle frequent UI state changes produced by dynamic Windows interfaces is represented by accessibility event throughput. Under controlled event generation workloads simulating rapid focus transitions, scroll events and notification updates across multiple running applications, ALO sustained approximately 1,050 events per second on Configuration~C and up to 1,234 events per second on Configuration~A, compared to NVDA's sustained throughput of 847 events per second under equivalent load. The temporal coalescing mechanism achieved an empirical reduction factor of approximately 3.7:1 between raw events received and events emitted for speech processing under typical workloads. Across all test configurations, the average processing latency per event was 23.4\,ms.

\subsection{System Resource Overhead}

Screen readers run continuously in the background throughout all computer use, making resource efficiency critical. During active navigation sessions, ALO consumed an average of 7.1\% CPU (peak 16.4\% during intensive event processing), compared to NVDA's 8.3\% average (peak 19.6\%) and JAWS's 9.1\% average (peak 22.8\%), a 14.5\% improvement in average load over NVDA (Table~\ref{tab:perfcomp}). The modest CPU advantage reflects ALO's coalescing filter reducing downstream processing work; under light navigation loads the .NET runtime and background thread pool bring ALO's consumption close to NVDA's. JAWS runs heavier due to its always-on background service processes despite its compiled C++ core. Memory usage is where ALO pays for its speed. At baseline, ALO holds around 318\,MB and climbs to about 387\,MB during heavy TTS activity compared to NVDA at 43.6\,MB (single process) and JAWS at 164.8\,MB (all background service processes combined). That difference comes directly from the pre-populated UIA property cache, the in-memory navigation index and the background thread structures that make zero-latency navigation queries possible. The footprint is stable: an eight-hour session showed memory growth below 1.2\% per hour with no COM object accumulation, so the number is high by design rather than by leak. Table~\ref{tab:perfcomp} summarizes the full comparative performance metrics across all three systems.

