\subsection{Speech Synthesis Performance}

We measured four aspects of speech output: how fast synthesis starts, how smoothly the system switches between languages, how accurately the output is understood and how it recovers when something goes wrong.

\subsubsection{Speech Synthesis Latency}

Speech synthesis latency measures the time interval between accessibility event detection and initiation of audio output. Bengali speech synthesis exhibited mean latency of 89.3\,ms ($\sigma = 15.7$\,ms) for short phrases (1-5 words) and 127.4\,ms ($\sigma = 23.2$\,ms) for longer sentences (10-15 words). English synthesis was a bit faster, averaging 76.8\,ms ($\sigma = 12.4$\,ms) for short phrases and 108.9\,ms ($\sigma = 19.6$\,ms) for longer ones. Bengali takes longer because there is more work happening before the audio even starts the text has to go through NFKC normalization, get checked against the Romanized Bengali lexicon and then be dispatched over the WebSocket bridge to the background synthesis engine. The 95th percentile latencies were 156\,ms for Bengali and 134\,ms for English, both within acceptable bounds for real-time interaction.

\subsubsection{Language Switching Performance}

Mixed-language interface elements require language boundary detection, segment routing and TTS engine switching. Language boundary identification via the sliding-window tokenization mechanism averaged 23.7\,ms ($\sigma = 8.3$\,ms) for typical mixed-content scenarios, while voice engine switching required approximately 105.0\,ms ($\sigma = 18.7$\,ms). For mixed sentences containing both Bengali and English segments, total processing time averaged 258.3\,ms ($\sigma = 42.4$\,ms), inclusive of segmentation, detection, lexicon lookup and dual-engine synthesis coordination. For comparison, NVDA's Bengali add-on incurs 134.6\,ms for the engine-switching step alone, without accounting for the preceding segmentation and detection stages; ALO's end-to-end switching cost of 105.0\,ms represents a 22.0\% reduction over that step, while covering a broader processing scope (Table~\ref{tab:perfcomp}).

\subsubsection{Intelligibility}

To assess output intelligibility, we ran synthesized speech through the Google Cloud Speech-to-Text API for English and the SUST-Bangla ASR model \cite{ahmad2021sust} for Bengali. We tested 200 sentences per language, keeping the test material entirely separate from anything used to build the Romanized Bengali lexicon so the scores would not be inflated by overlap. Bengali synthesis came in at 91.2\% word-level recognition accuracy; human Bengali recordings of the same sentences scored 94.7\%, giving a sense of the gap still left to close. English synthesis reached 96.8\%, just under the 98.1\% human reference. On prosody, the synthesized Bengali fundamental frequency sat at 187\,Hz ($\sigma = 34$\,Hz) for male voices and 276\,Hz ($\sigma = 41$\,Hz) for female voices, both within the range expected of native Bengali speakers. Two independent annotators reviewed 100 synthesized sentences for sentence boundary pauses and agreed on correct placement in 87.3\% of cases.

\subsubsection{Failure Recovery Behavior}

System resilience was assessed across 30 simulated failure events per failure type on each of the three hardware configurations (90 trials total per type). Engine failure was simulated by programmatically terminating the Bengali synthesis process mid-utterance; audio resource conflict was simulated by forcing exclusive audio device acquisition from a second process. When the primary synthesis engine became unavailable, ALO detected the failure, fell back to a SAPI-based synthesis path and resumed audio output in approximately 340\,ms ($\sigma = 67$\,ms). Audio resource conflicts were resolved with an average recovery time of 1.23\,s ($\sigma = 0.34$\,s), during which non-speech cues preserved navigation context. Under memory pressure exceeding 90\%, ALO automatically reduced synthesis complexity to preserve functional output; recovery to full-quality synthesis completed in 2.1\,s ($\sigma = 0.8$\,s) with a 97.3\% success rate across all trials.

\subsection{Task-Based Comparative Assessment}

Standardized Windows interaction tasks were used in Study~B to compare ALO with NVDA and JAWS across three categories: document navigation, mixed-language text input and Bengali web browsing. All fifteen participants completed every task on all three systems.

For English-only document navigation, the differences between systems were present but modest. NVDA and JAWS both handle structured English content reasonably well and participants familiar with either tool navigated comfortably. ALO's indexed virtual buffer reduced the number of keystrokes needed to reach a target heading, which participants noticed, but the gap was not dramatic for purely English content.

The picture changed substantially once Bengali entered the picture. Both NVDA and JAWS rely on explicit language markup in the source document or application to trigger any language-aware synthesis behaviour. In practice, neither Zoom chat messages, WhatsApp notifications nor Bengali government web portals carry such markup, so both tools fell back to their configured default synthesizer and attempted to render Bengali text as English. The resulting output ranged from heavily mispronounced to completely unintelligible and participants using those tools had to either mentally decode the garbled audio or manually switch synthesizers between segments, a process that interrupts the interaction flow entirely.

ALO's bilingual pipeline handled the same content without any user intervention. Language boundary detection via sliding-window tokenization averaged 23.7\,ms per segment and the full engine-switching step completed in approximately 105.0\,ms end-to-end. NVDA's Bengali add-on, when present, requires around 134.6\,ms for the engine switch alone, before any segmentation or detection has occurred. This gap is not incidental. In a task involving a message thread or web page with a dozen or more Bengali segments, a consistent 30\,ms per-boundary saving accumulates into a perceptibly smoother experience. The benchmark latencies measured in Section~\ref{sec:empirical} therefore translate directly into task-level differences: participants using ALO moved through Bengali content at a pace comparable to how they experience English content on familiar tools, whereas NVDA and JAWS introduced a noticeable pause or outright synthesizer failure at each language boundary.

Romanized Bengali, the Latin-script Bengali common in WhatsApp and social messaging, was the sharpest point of contrast. Neither NVDA nor JAWS have any mechanism to detect or handle it; both tools read words like ``ami'' or ``kemon acho'' as English and produce output that bears no relation to the intended meaning. ALO's lexicon-matching stage recognises these tokens and routes them to the Bengali synthesis engine, which participants found immediately useful given how frequently Romanized Bengali appears in everyday digital communication in Bangladesh. Across the mixed-language input and Bengali web browsing tasks, participants using ALO were able to complete their work without stopping to correct or reinterpret synthesizer output, a pattern that was consistent across the cohort and across all three hardware configurations.

\subsection{Long-Term System Stability}

To assess system stability, ALO was subjected to continuous operational testing over extended sessions. Memory usage stabilized at approximately 324\,MB ($\sigma = 18.4$\,MB) after initial launch, reflecting the fully populated UIA cache and navigation index and grew very little beyond that over long periods. The COM lifecycle tracker recycled over 99\% of acquired COM objects across all tested sessions, with no memory leaks detected. Event listener cleanup achieved 100\% success across all accessibility events processed and TTS processing resources were correctly released after each synthesis operation. Audio feedback remained consistently responsive across all stability sessions.

\subsection{User Satisfaction with Visually Impaired Participants}

\subsubsection{Comparative Preference Analysis}

Preference results were unambiguous for Bengali tasks. Every participant chose ALO over NVDA and JAWS for reading Bengali text and for mixed Bengali-English content. Thirteen out of fifteen participants (86.7\%) preferred ALO for general system navigation and Bengali web browsing. For English-only content, eight out of fifteen participants (53.3\%) preferred ALO, highlighting that ALO's primary advantage lies in the Bengali and bilingual domain where existing tools are inadequate. No participant preferred NVDA or JAWS for any Bengali-language task.

\subsubsection{Qualitative Feedback}

Think-aloud sessions and structured interviews revealed recurring themes. The Bangla-specific TTS pipeline was identified as the system's most significant innovation: all participants found that ALO's Bengali pronunciation was substantially superior to NVDA and JAWS. Multiple participants independently reported more responsive keyboard navigation, easier language switching between Bengali and English and quicker navigation for Bengali content.

Primary areas for improvement were the learning curve for the full feature set, occasional latency during Bengali synthesis under high system load and the absence of Braille display support. Of the fifteen participants, thirteen (86.7\%) indicated they would use ALO as their primary screen reader for Bengali content, while the remaining two indicated they would selectively use ALO for Bengali applications alongside NVDA for English-dominant workflows. All fifteen said they would take part in follow-up testing.

\subsubsection{Application-Specific Feedback}

Visually impaired testers particularly valued the Zoom, WhatsApp and browser plugins, citing these as the most difficult application contexts to navigate with general-purpose screen readers. Within Zoom, the ability to send meeting reactions and navigate chat history by sender or time without losing focus context was cited as a substantial usability improvement. In WhatsApp specifically, several participants mentioned that one of their regular frustrations with NVDA was losing track of where they were when switching between the chat list and an open conversation. ALO keeps that context intact, so moving back and forth no longer means starting over. For Word and Excel, what stood out was being able to jump directly to a heading or a table column header rather than arrowing through everything line by line something participants said saves a lot of time in longer documents \cite{zong2022rich}. A few participants who had struggled with NVDA's learning curve also noted that ALO's approach of surfacing shortcuts gradually as they used the system made it much easier to get going without sitting down with a manual first \cite{chen2026struggle}.

