\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

The Bengali TTS engine handles general vocabulary well but struggles with dialectal variation and domain-specific terms medical and legal text in particular comes out mispronounced often enough to be a practical limitation, consistent with what neural TTS systems produce for low-resource language variants. The Romanized Bengali lexicon covers approximately 5,000 common terms; tokens not in the lexicon are silently routed to the English synthesis engine, which may produce incorrect output for uncovered Romanized Bengali words.

ALO's pre-loaded UIA cache and in-memory navigation index require 300-400\,MB of RAM, which is substantially more than NVDA or JAWS. This is a deliberate speed-memory trade-off. Given that ALO peaks at approximately 387\,MB and the host OS and target applications typically consume a further 400-600\,MB, systems with less than 1\,GB of available RAM are at risk of paging under simultaneous load, which may concern users on shared or constrained hardware. Event coalescing trades some immediacy for fewer interruptions and can introduce minor delays of up to 50~ms in certain situations. In fast-moving interfaces such as live code editors or real-time dashboards, this delay is occasionally perceptible. On Configuration~C hardware, rapid plugin switching or handling high-frequency UI Automation events from applications like Zoom occasionally produced speech latencies approaching 150~ms under peak load; general navigation and speech were not affected under normal usage conditions.

ALO currently runs only on Windows because it depends on UI Automation and COM APIs and supporting other platforms would require either substantial porting effort or a platform-abstraction layer. Applications built on Flutter, React Native, or Electron sometimes expose incomplete or malformed UI Automation trees, providing insufficient accessibility metadata for reliable navigation. The heuristic fallback layer mitigates this impact, but element labelling accuracy is reduced compared to natively accessible applications. Although ALO's modular architecture promotes maintainability, it adds inter-component communication overhead that is measurable on older hardware (Configuration~C), where garbage collection pressure \cite{bacon2004unified} can cause audio feedback delays greater than 200~ms under extreme system load.

