\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

ALO addresses multilingual interaction, high-frequency event processing and extensibility for Bengali-English users on Windows. A three-stage bilingual pipeline combining Unicode NFKC normalization, sliding-window tokenization and a 5,000-entry Romanized Bengali lexicon switches between Bengali, English and mixed content without user intervention. Priority-based temporal event coalescing reduced speech interruptions by a 3.7:1 ratio while preserving priority alerts. A sandboxed plugin architecture keeps application-specific extensions isolated from the core and deterministic two-tier COM lifecycle management eliminates the memory leaks common in long-running COM-based integrations. Empirical evaluation confirms that the architecture delivers measurable improvements across all tested dimensions. ALO achieves event-to-speech latency 8--11\% lower than NVDA across standard interaction scenarios, maintains accessible event-processing throughput over 1,200 events per second and reduces average CPU consumption by approximately 14.5\% compared to NVDA under dynamic load conditions. At the 95th percentile, navigation latency stays below 140\,ms, within acceptable bounds for responsive real-time engagement. Bengali TTS initialization is 25.0\% faster than NVDA's Bengali add-on and language-switching overhead is reduced by 22.0\%. Task-based evaluation with 15 experienced screen reader users confirmed consistent advantages across all task categories, most pronounced in Bengali text-input and mixed-language web browsing where NVDA and JAWS produced unintelligible output at language boundaries that ALO resolved automatically. User preference analysis confirmed that 86.7\% of participants preferred ALO for Bengali and mixed-language interaction, while all participants preferred it specifically for mixed Bengali-English content. All components required to reproduce these results installer, source code, benchmark logs and evaluation data are documented in supplementary Sections~2 and~7\footref{fn:supplementary}.

Although Bengali has over 230 million speakers, the tools available to them especially for assistive technology lag far behind what exists for English or other Western languages. For visually impaired people in Bangladesh specifically, that gap is not just an inconvenience; it affects whether someone can read a government form, hold a job that involves a computer, or take part in an online class \cite{era2026evaluating, motahar2025accessibility, munna2025advancing}. ALO is a direct response to that. When a screen reader can actually handle the way people in Bangladesh write and speak switching between Bengali and English mid-sentence, or typing Bengali words in Latin script it stops being a workaround and starts being a tool people can genuinely rely on. Because the plugin architecture keeps application-specific logic separate from the core, adding support for a new locally relevant application does not require touching anything that already works.

Future work will focus on expanding the Romanized Bengali lexicon, incorporating lightweight statistical disambiguation for ambiguous tokens, improving Bengali TTS pronunciation for domain-specific terminology and conducting a longitudinal field study with a larger participant cohort. Braille display integration and cross-platform accessibility API support are planned extensions. The bilingual routing design is not Bengali-specific; the same pipeline architecture can be extended to other code-switching pairs common in multilingual digital environments, including Hindi-English, Urdu-English and Tamil-English, offering a replicable path for improving digital accessibility for other under-resourced language communities.

